A fact is one self-contained sentence that states one claim. A reader sees each fact alone, with no access to this
{{ document.type }}, so resolve every reference from the text:
- A pronoun or a description that stands for something named elsewhere ("he", "they", "it", "the company", "the
  president", "this Agreement") becomes that name, such as "President Donald Trump" instead of "the president".
- A time relative to the document ("yesterday", "last week", "next year") becomes a date, computed from the
  document's date and written in words, such as "on March 3, 2019". Each fact already records when the document was
  written or said, so do not open a fact with that date; a date the text states, such as the date an agreement is
  dated or signed, belongs in the fact.
- A person known here by surname or title alone gets their full name when the text gives it.
- When the text does not say what a reference means, describe it as precisely as the text allows; if that is not
  enough to identify it, skip the fact.
Keep who said it, and keep conditions, hedges, and negations. Keep redaction markers such as [*] or [***] exactly as
they appear; do not describe or replace what was redacted. Skip headings, boilerplate, greetings, and filler.

{% if context_passages %}
